As someone who's read a lot of Irish/Scots/Welsh mythology
(well, also some Finnish, Chinese, the standard Greco-Roman
stuff, Chinese, some Amerind and Japanese) (in case you were
wondering, Indian is next, and then African) from which the
"elves" were copied (they were copied from the Irish/Welsh/
Scots, not all the other stuff; that's just what I've been
reading) a weapon of "drain life" would be somewhat out
of character or perhaps even downright evil to "elves."

Now, something to ressurect you from the dead, so that you
can kill again, would be in character, but in this instance
you have to do the killing, not the sword.

I agree with you about Tolkien, partially because of the
kludgy way he tries to cram some of the poetry of Celtic
myth into the manicheistic framework of Christianity (and/or
the Norse world view) in order to make the latter more
attractive.

Although I haven't read it in a long time, though, you might
enjoy C.S. Lewis' _Till We Have Faces_. He seems to succeed
where Tolkien fails.

